course: dictionary
lesson: 12489
title: Showing it is true
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Demonstrably tells you that something can be clearly shown or proved. It is not just what someone thinks. «The results demonstrably show that the plant grows faster in sunlight.» The results are facts you can point to, so the claim is proved.
explain: Look for the clues around the word. Ask: is there evidence here, like measurements, tests or photos? «The bridge is demonstrably safe, because the engineers tested every beam.» The tests are the evidence, so safe is backed up by proof.
explain: Try a swap to check your meaning. Replace demonstrably with clearly shown or proved. If the sentence still makes sense, you have it. «The team was demonstrably better» means you could show it, for example by the scores.
fact: Demonstrably means in a way that can be clearly shown or proved.
fact: Demonstrably comes from the word demonstrate, which means to show clearly.
fact: «Demonstrably true» means you can prove it is true with facts.
fact: «Demonstrably false» means you can prove it is wrong with facts.
fact: Writers use demonstrably in science and arguments to show that a claim is backed by evidence, not just opinion.
exercise: «The experiment demonstrably proves that sound travels through water.» What does demonstrably mean here? | *Clearly shown or proved | Loudly and in a noisy way | Only sometimes | Guessed by someone who likes water
exercise: «That idea is demonstrably false, because we have the photos.» Why does the writer say demonstrably? | *Because the photos prove the idea is wrong | Because the photos are old | Because it is a long word | Because the writer likes the idea
exercise: Which phrase means you can clearly prove something is better? | hardly better | better, I think | *demonstrably better | sort of better
test:
«Our tests demonstrably show that the new fence keeps the sheep in.» What does demonstrably mean? | Clearly shown with proof | Friendly and kind | Quickly and without care | *Clearly shown with proof
test:
Ella says, «I think the sky is green.» Jack replies, «That is demonstrably false, look at the photo.» What does Jack mean? | He means the sky is a lovely colour | He means he is not sure what colour it is | *He means a photo proves the sky is not green | He means Ella should look at the photo more
exam:
«The data demonstrably shows that the medicine works, so the doctors agree.» What does demonstrably mean? | Quietly and with doubt | Only in dreams | *Clearly shown and proved by evidence | Without any reason at all
